  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1111 AM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nNorthern Colorado remains on the edge of a broad upper level ridge\ntoday, with weak zonal flow aloft. The little moisture aloft has\nbeen enough for a few cirrus clouds closer to the state border.\nHigh temperatures are still expected to warm into the upper 60s\nacross most of the plains this afternoon with light winds.\n\nTomorrow will feature much of the same weather, with temperatures\nagain in the upper 60s. A shortwave moving from the northern\nRockies into the central Great Plains will also drag a weak cold\nfront through the region. Some gusty winds will be possible across\nthe northern/northeastern portions of our forecast area during the\nafternoon into Tuesday night. That could lead to some spotty\nelevated/near critical fire weather conditions across the plains,\ngenerally north of Highway 34. \n\nTemperatures are expected to be a few degrees cooler on Wednesday,\nthough highs should still reach the low/mid 60s across the plains,\nwhich is several degrees above normal. By the latter half of the \nweek, a stronger upper level ridge is expected to build across the\nFour Corners region, leading to warmer and dry weather. It \nwouldn't be surprising to see one or both of Thursday/Friday to \nend up in the low 70s. \n\nThe next potential for precipitation in our CWA will come this\nweekend, as guidance tries to push a weak trough across the region\nby Saturday. This system won't feature any significant synoptic\nscale ascent and only some light rain and snow showers are\nexpected across the high country during the weekend.\n\nQuiet weather looks to continue into early next week, with hints\nof a cooler/more active forecast pattern in the 8-10 day period.",
